MobileGlues: the BC encoder's GPU buffers made once and reused, and empty levels filled from one of them.

Fast start's cache loads every atlas whole, and each went to the encoder as one job, in GPU buffers its size made anew
for every batch; the driver kept the old ones until the GPU had used them, and copied every strip of an empty level
from memory into GPU memory of its own the same way. On a Xiaomi 12T the game's GPU memory went to 2.1 GB for 1.3 GB
of textures (Space Age), 1.3 GB for 0.6 (the base game). Now a job goes in strips of block rows, at most 4 MB of the
game's blocks and 4 MB encoded a batch, into two pairs of buffers made once and used in turn, each waited for (a
fence) before it's filled again; an empty level's rows come from a buffer of empty blocks made once for its format,
where uploads from a buffer land right. The base game's normal start on the 12T: 2.18 GB at its peak -> 1.87.
Batches are 4 MB, and the buffers are let go once the encoder has been idle 3 s: it encodes nothing more once the
atlases are made, and they were 64 MB of GPU memory the game could use.
